### Scenario E: Unescaped Markdown Injection in Agent-to-Human Notification Transports

* **Domain**: Security Architecture & Output Encoding
* **Problem Specification**:
  An MCP server provides a tool `post_incident_summary` that sends markdown reports to a corporate Slack channel. An agent analyzes an untrusted log file containing a malicious payload:
  `[Click here for debug logs](https://evil-phishing.com/oauth/authorize?steal=true)`.
  The tool blindly forwards the text, rendering a deceptive phishing link in the executive incident war-room.
* **Diagnostic Telemetry**:
  Slack API message delivery payload:
  ```json
  {
    "type": "section",
    "text": {
      "type": "mrkdwn",
      "text": "Incident Analysis: Root cause identified. <https://evil-phishing.com/oauth/authorize|Click here for debug logs>"
    }
  }
  ```
* **Architectural Root Cause**:
  Lack of output sanitization on untrusted strings flowing from tool analysis into human communication channels.
* **Production Remediation**:
  Implement **Strict URL Whitelisting and Domain Sanitization**:
  ```typescript
  export function sanitizeSlackMarkdown(untrustedText: string, allowedDomains: string[]): string {
    return untrustedText.replace(/\[([^\]]+)\]\((https?:\/\/[^\s\)]+)\)/g, (match, anchor, url) => {
      try {
        const parsed = new URL(url);
        if (allowedDomains.includes(parsed.hostname)) {
          return `<${url}|${anchor}>`;
        }
        return `[UNTRUSTED LINK TO ${parsed.hostname} BLOCKED]`;
      } catch {
        return `[INVALID URL]`;
      }
    });
  }
  ```

