### Scenario H: Token-Bucket Rate Limiting Starvation in Batch Refactoring

* **Domain**: Performance & Rate Governance
* **Problem Specification**:
  An autonomous batch refactoring swarm with 12 worker agents attempts to query GitHub MCP server endpoints to inspect 200 repository branches. Within 10 seconds, GitHub returns `429 Too Many Requests`. The naive backoff algorithm causes all 12 workers to sleep for 60 seconds and wake up at the exact same millisecond, triggering a **Thundering Herd** that strikes rate limits again.
* **Diagnostic Telemetry**:
  ```
  [09:00:00] Worker 1..12 -> 429 Too Many Requests (Rate limit remaining: 0)
  [09:01:00] Worker 1..12 wake up simultaneously
  [09:01:00] Worker 1..12 -> 429 Too Many Requests (Thundering herd)
  [09:02:00] Pipeline locked in oscillation
  ```
* **Architectural Root Cause**:
  Lack of randomized jitter in exponential backoff algorithms and absence of a centralized token dispenser across distributed workers.
* **Production Remediation**:
  Deploy **Full Jitter Exponential Backoff** ($T_{\text{sleep}} = \text{random}(0, \min(T_{\text{max}}, T_{\text{base}} \times 2^{\text{attempt}}))$):
  ```typescript
  export async function executeWithJitter<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, maxRetries = 5): Promise<T> {
    let attempt = 0;
    const baseMs = 1000;
    const maxMs = 30000;

    while (attempt < maxRetries) {
      try {
        return await fn();
      } catch (err: any) {
        if (err.status !== 429 && err.code !== 'RATE_LIMITED') throw err;
        attempt++;
        const backoff = Math.min(maxMs, baseMs * Math.pow(2, attempt));
        const jitteredSleep = Math.random() * backoff;
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, jitteredSleep));
      }
    }
    throw new Error('Maximum retry attempts exceeded under rate limiting');
  }
  ```

