### Scenario P: Broken Invariant in Distributed Redlock Implementation

* **Domain**: Distributed Concurrency & Consensus
* **Problem Specification**:
  A multi-agent swarm uses Redis with the Redlock algorithm to synchronize database migrations. During a spike in VM CPU steal time on the Redis nodes, two independent worker agents acquire the *same* exclusive lock simultaneously, corrupting database tables.
* **Diagnostic Telemetry**:
  ```
  Worker 1: Acquired lock 'lock:db_migration' at T = 100.00s (TTL: 10s)
  Redis Node B: Experiences 8-second CPU pause
  Worker 2: Requests lock 'lock:db_migration' at T = 111.00s -> Acquired!
  Worker 1: Still executing migration assuming it holds exclusive lock!
  ```
* **Architectural Root Cause**:
  The lock validity time was computed without factoring in clock drift and elapsed acquisition time. By the time Worker 1 finished acquiring the lock across all Redis instances, the effective lock validity had expired.
* **Production Remediation**:
  Always compute **Effective Validity Time** ($T_{\text{validity}} = \text{TTL} - \Delta t - \text{Drift}$):
  ```typescript
  export function isLockValid(ttlMs: number, elapsedAcquisitionMs: number, driftFactor = 0.01): boolean {
    const clockDriftMs = Math.round(ttlMs * driftFactor) + 2;
    const effectiveValidityMs = ttlMs - elapsedAcquisitionMs - clockDriftMs;
    return effectiveValidityMs > 0;
  }
  ```

